#version 460 // GL_EXT_ray_query is written against GLSL 4.60 (see shaders/rt_shadow.comp)
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : require
// Shading a hit needs the geometry, and the only thing that reaches it without a descriptor is its
// device address (see shaders/hit_shading.glsl): a buffer reference, plus the 64-bit arithmetic that
// addresses it.
#extension GL_EXT_buffer_reference : require
#extension GL_EXT_shader_explicit_arithmetic_types_int64 : require
// The bindless texture array (set 0 binding 1) is indexed by the material's own indices, exactly as
// shaders/surface.glsl indexes it.
#extension GL_EXT_nonuniform_qualifier : enable

/**
 * @file shaders/ssgi_spec.comp
 * @brief The glossy lobe: one GGX-sampled reflection ray per invocation, added into the tracer's output.
 * @ingroup shaders
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS. Until this pass every indirect term in the engine was diffuse. The lighting stage
 * adds a SPECULAR ambient too - `ibl_specular * F * ao`, the split-sum lookup of the prefiltered
 * environment along the reflection direction - and that lookup knows nothing but the sky: a metal panel
 * inside a room reflects the sky, and so does a wet floor, and so does the brass on a statue. The tracer
 * next door traces 2 cosine-weighted rays per pixel and can answer for the room; this pass asks it the
 * other question, "what does the environment along the REFLECTION direction actually contain", and
 * answers it with the same machinery: trace, and if the ray lands on geometry, shade that geometry.
 *
 * IT IS A REPLACEMENT, NOT AN ADDITION, and the arithmetic of that is the design. What the lighting
 * stage already added is `ibl_specular * F * ao`, and what this pass writes is
 * `ao * (1/N) * sum_i L_i * F` where `L_i` is either the radiance a ray found or - when it found
 * nothing - `ibl_specular` ITSELF, the same prefiltered lookup along the mirror direction, MINUS that
 * same term. So:
 *
 *   * a ray that MISSES contributes exactly the value the lighting stage used, so the correction is
 *     exactly zero, and
 *   * a ray that HITS contributes the radiance of the surface it landed on,
 *
 * and the subtraction happens HERE, at the texel, BEFORE the joint-bilateral filter sees either side.
 * That placement is the whole result of this step and it was chosen by measurement: subtracting the
 * lighting stage's term in the spatial filter instead (which is where the DIFFUSE ambient is removed)
 * subtracts a CENTRE-pixel value from a FILTERED one, and the difference is not small. Measured on an
 * isolated model whose rays reach nothing, so that the correct answer is "the frame does not change at
 * all": mean +0.0255 with 4.4% of pixels off by more than 4/255 and a worst pixel of 103 that way
 * against 0 pixels and the same SHA256 this way - BIT-IDENTICAL to the feature being off, with the
 * filter in the loop and with it bypassed. docs/gi_hit_shading.md's L2.3 section has both tables.
 *
 * WHAT THAT COSTS, measured rather than argued: this image is no longer a pure radiance, it is a
 * radiance plus the specular correction, and the multi-bounce feedback re-emits whatever is here at a
 * hit. That is the property the Level 1 work established and this pass is the one place it can be given
 * up safely, for a reason that is structural: the lobe requires `ssgi_hit_shading`, and with hit shading
 * on every hit is answered by `shade_hit` before the code that would add the feedback is reached (see
 * shaders/ssgi.comp's rt_hit_radiance - "the ambient a shaded hit carries already IS its indirect
 * estimate"). Measured: `ssgi_bounce` 0 against 0.5 is a byte-exact no-op on the material sweep, before
 * and after this change. The DIFFUSE term cannot be handled this way, and is not: its image has to stay
 * a radiance because the bounce is live whenever hit shading is off.
 *
 * The fallback deliberately does NOT sample the environment along the SAMPLED direction: the prefiltered
 * cube is already the GGX cone's average, so averaging several of its values would filter it twice and
 * widen the lobe. A miss re-reads the cone's own answer; only a hit is a point sample of the cone.
 *
 * WHAT IT SHARES WITH THE TRACER, and why the output is the tracer's own image. This pass does not own
 * an image: it READS the raw trace and ADDS its correction to it (imageLoad / imageStore, with a
 * compute-to-compute barrier between the two dispatches). Two things fall out of that, and each one is
 * the reason it is done this way rather than with an image of its own:
 *   1. the denoiser needs no new input. The temporal resolve's neighbourhood clamp is taken from the
 *      "current frame's raw trace", and if the specular lobe lived in a second image that clamp would
 *      box in the diffuse-only range and reject the specular from the history - the accumulation would
 *      then never average the very signal that needs averaging most;
 *   2. nothing about the descriptor sets changes: the set the tracer binds already has this image as a
 *      read-write storage image at binding 6, and this pass binds the SAME two sets.
 *
 * THE FALLBACK IS EXACT ONLY WHEN THE RAY MISSES, and the hit path is where the approximation is:
 * `shade_hit` answers with the hit surface's own outgoing radiance (its sun with a shadow ray, its
 * split-sum ambient and its emissive), which is a POINT sample of a cone a prefiltered lookup would have
 * averaged - at high roughness a single glossy ray is a noisy estimate of a wide lobe, and
 * `[render] ssgi_specular_rays` is the knob that buys samples (measured: one ray is already converged
 * after the denoisers). Two further limits belong to the hit path and are inherited from
 * shaders/hit_shading.glsl: a MASK surface is solid to the ray and a skinned or morphed mesh is shaded in
 * its bind pose, because the structures hold what the build read. A BACK-FACE hit (a ray that ended
 * inside geometry) is treated as a miss: there is no surface there to reflect.
 *
 * WHAT THE INCLUDER MUST PROVIDE (see shaders/hit_shading.glsl): the top level structure (set 0 binding
 * 16) and the irradiance cube (binding 3) are declared below, and the camera (binding 0) because the
 * primary surface's own view vector is needed for its Fresnel term. Everything else - the material table,
 * the texture array, the light UBO, the prefiltered environment and the BRDF LUT - comes with the include,
 * as does `shade_hit` itself and the shared split-sum specular of shaders/ibl_specular.glsl.
 */

layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8) in;

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform CameraUBO {
    mat4 view;
    mat4 proj;
    vec4 camera_pos;
} camera;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3) uniform samplerCube irradiance_sampler;
layout(set = 0, binding = 16) uniform accelerationStructureEXT tlas;

// The G-buffer set (see runtime::ensure_gbuffer_descriptors), the same set the tracer binds: the stored
// surface this pass reflects from, and the raw trace it adds its lobe to. Binding 6 is the tracer's own
// output image, declared READ-WRITE here rather than writeonly - that is the whole mechanism (see the
// file header).
layout(set = 1, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D gbuffer_albedo;   // RGBA8: albedo.rgb + metallic
layout(set = 1, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D gbuffer_normal;   // RGBA16F: world normal.xyz + roughness
layout(set = 1, binding = 2) uniform sampler2D gbuffer_material; // RGBA8: .b = the material's baked AO
layout(set = 1, binding = 3) uniform sampler2D gbuffer_depth;    // the pass's own 1x depth
layout(set = 1, binding = 6, rgba16f) uniform image2D gi_trace;  // the raw trace: read, added to, written back

#include "hit_shading.glsl"

layout(push_constant) uniform SsgiSpecPush {
    mat4 inv_view_proj; // clip -> world, the same block and the same reconstruction the tracer uses
    // x = the ray length in world units (the tracer's own scene-relative radius), y = glossy rays per
    // pixel, z = free, w = a per-frame counter, which the ray sequence needs: a fixed sequence would feed
    // a temporal filter the same error every frame instead of an average of different ones. The shadow
    // ray's bias scale is x, exactly as the tracer passes it to the same function.
    vec4 params;
    // z/w = the instance table's device address in two 32-bit halves (the shape every pass in this
    // project pushes an address in), and 0 is the switch: without a table a hit cannot be shaded, so the
    // pass leaves the frame alone rather than replacing the environment with nothing.
    vec4 table;
} pc;

/** @brief a PCG-style integer hash: the ray sequence's only source of randomness
 *  @note the tracer's own (shaders/ssgi.comp); duplicated because a shader cannot include another
 *        shader's entry point, and the two must not share a definition through a .glsl because they are
 *        compiled into different modules with different push blocks */
uint hash_u32(uint x) {
    x ^= x >> 16;
    x *= 0x7feb352du;
    x ^= x >> 15;
    x *= 0x846ca68bu;
    x ^= x >> 16;
    return x;
}

/** @brief the next uniform in [0,1) from @p state (a hash chain, so no table and no texture) */
float next_uniform(inout uint state) {
    state = hash_u32(state);
    return float(state) * (1.0 / 4294967296.0);
}

/** @brief the 64-bit device address held in two push lanes */
uint64_t lane_address(vec2 halves) {
    return (uint64_t(floatBitsToUint(halves.y)) << 32u) | uint64_t(floatBitsToUint(halves.x));
}

/**
 * @brief a half-vector drawn from the GGX/Trowbridge-Reitz distribution, for a roughness
 * @param u two uniforms in [0,1)
 * @param n the surface normal the lobe is built around (any space; the result is in the same one)
 * @param roughness the material's perceptual roughness
 * @return a unit half-vector around @p n
 *
 * The NDF the prefiltered environment chain is baked with, sampled by its own inversion: that is what
 * makes "average the radiance found along these directions" the same integral the CPU prefilter computed
 * for the lighting stage, rather than a differently-shaped lobe that would disagree with it at every
 * roughness. The parameterisation is the standard one (PBRT's, as every real-time implementation uses).
 */
vec3 importance_sample_ggx(vec2 u, vec3 n, float roughness) {
    const float a = roughness * roughness;
    const float a2 = a * a;
    const float phi = 6.283185307179586 * u.x;
    const float cos_theta = sqrt((1.0 - u.y) / max(1.0 + (a2 - 1.0) * u.y, 1e-6));
    const float sin_theta = sqrt(max(1.0 - cos_theta * cos_theta, 0.0));
    // the same orthonormal basis the tracer builds for its cosine lobe, so a lobe's shape does not depend
    // on which helper sampled it
    const vec3 helper = abs(n.x) < 0.9 ? vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0) : vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
    const vec3 tangent = normalize(cross(helper, n));
    const vec3 bitangent = cross(n, tangent);
    return normalize(tangent * (sin_theta * cos(phi)) + bitangent * (sin_theta * sin(phi)) + n * cos_theta);
}

void main() {
    const ivec2 gi_extent = imageSize(gi_trace);
    const ivec2 pixel = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
    if (pixel.x >= gi_extent.x || pixel.y >= gi_extent.y) {
        return; // the dispatch is rounded up to the workgroup size
    }

    const int rays = int(pc.params.y);
    const uint64_t table = lane_address(pc.table.zw);
    if (rays <= 0 || table == 0u) {
        // Nothing to do, and NOTHING IS WRITTEN: the tracer's estimate stands untouched, which is what
        // makes a frame with the feature off byte-identical by construction rather than by arithmetic.
        return;
    }

    const vec2 uv = (vec2(pixel) + 0.5) / vec2(gi_extent);
    const float depth_ndc = texture(gbuffer_depth, uv).r;
    if (depth_ndc >= 1.0) {
        return; // background: the tracer wrote 0 here and the lighting stage shaded no surface here
    }

    // This pixel's surface. The world position comes from the same inverse projection the tracer and the
    // lighting stage use, so all three agree about where the surface is.
    const vec4 clip = vec4(uv * 2.0 - 1.0, depth_ndc, 1.0);
    const vec4 world_h = pc.inv_view_proj * clip;
    const vec3 world_pos = world_h.xyz / world_h.w;
    const vec3 normal = normalize(texture(gbuffer_normal, uv).xyz);
    const float roughness = clamp(texture(gbuffer_normal, uv).w, 0.045, 1.0);
    const vec4 albedo_metallic = texture(gbuffer_albedo, uv);
    const float ao = texture(gbuffer_material, uv).b;

    const vec3 v = normalize(camera.camera_pos.xyz - world_pos);
    const vec3 f0 = mix(vec3(0.04), albedo_metallic.rgb, albedo_metallic.a);
    // WHAT THE LIGHTING STAGE ALREADY ADDED for this surface, from the SAME expressions
    // (shaders/ibl_specular.glsl) in the same association order - `ibl * fresnel * ao`, left to right.
    // This value is what makes the pass a REPLACEMENT rather than an addition: it is what the estimate
    // below converges to when a ray finds no geometry, and it is SUBTRACTED here rather than removed
    // again downstream (see the store at the end, and the header for why that is the exact option).
    const vec3 ibl_specular = ibl_specular_radiance(normal, v, roughness);
    const vec3 fresnel = ibl_specular_fresnel(normal, v, roughness, f0, 1.0);
    const vec3 already_added = (ibl_specular * fresnel) * ao;

    uint state = hash_u32(uint(pixel.x) + uint(pixel.y) * 4096u + uint(pc.params.w) * 2654435761u);
    vec3 radiance_sum = vec3(0.0);
    for (int r = 0; r < rays; ++r) {
        const vec3 h = importance_sample_ggx(vec2(next_uniform(state), next_uniform(state)), normal, roughness);
        const vec3 dir = reflect(-v, h);
        if (dot(dir, normal) <= 0.0) {
            // The sampled half-vector put the reflection below the surface: there is no geometry to reach
            // in that direction, so this sample is the environment's - exactly like a miss (see the file
            // header for why a miss does not sample along the sampled direction).
            radiance_sum += ibl_specular;
            continue;
        }
        const vec3 origin = world_pos + normal * (pc.params.x * 0.02);
        rayQueryEXT query;
        rayQueryInitializeEXT(query, tlas, gl_RayFlagsOpaqueEXT, 0xFF, origin, 0.01, dir, pc.params.x);
        while (rayQueryProceedEXT(query)) {
        }
        if (rayQueryGetIntersectionTypeEXT(query, true) == gl_RayQueryCommittedIntersectionNoneEXT) {
            radiance_sum += ibl_specular; // nothing within the ray's length: the environment answers
            continue;
        }
        if (!rayQueryGetIntersectionFrontFaceEXT(query, true)) {
            // A back face: the ray was inside geometry, and a reflection cannot carry light from a
            // solid's interior. The environment is the conservative answer, not the shaded one.
            radiance_sum += ibl_specular;
            continue;
        }
        // The radiance LEAVING the surface the ray landed on, shaded from its own geometry - the same
        // call the tracer makes, with the direction towards whoever is asking (here: back along the ray,
        // because the asking surface is the reflection's origin).
        const float hit_t = rayQueryGetIntersectionTEXT(query, true);
        const vec3 hit_world = origin + hit_t * dir;
        vec3 shaded = vec3(0.0);
        if (shade_hit(query, hit_world, dir, -dir, table, pc.params.x, shaded)) {
            radiance_sum += shaded;
        } else {
            radiance_sum += ibl_specular;
        }
    }

    // The BRDF weight is applied once, to the average, and the term the lighting stage already added is
    // taken off HERE - before anything filters this image - rather than by a subtraction in the spatial
    // filter after the fact. That is the difference between an exact replacement and an approximate one:
    // a ray that finds no geometry makes this value EXACTLY zero (`(ibl*F)*ao - (ibl*F)*ao`, the same
    // expression twice), and adding an exact zero to a texel is exact, so such a frame is bit-identical
    // to the feature being off even with the filter in the loop. The cost is that this image is no longer
    // a pure radiance: it is a radiance plus a bookkeeping term, which is what the multi-bounce feedback
    // re-emits at a hit (see the file header and docs/gi_hit_shading.md's L2.3 section for the A/B).
    const vec3 traced = ((radiance_sum / float(rays)) * fresnel) * ao;
    imageStore(gi_trace, pixel, imageLoad(gi_trace, pixel) + vec4(traced - already_added, 0.0));
}
